1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,120
Most of us will work until we are sixty-five

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:06,120
But in America and many developed countries,

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,160
some people retire at thirty,

4
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,600
or forty

5
00:00:10,194 --> 00:00:12,504
This is the story of the FIRE movement,

6
00:00:12,504 --> 00:00:14,744
through the journey of a fictional character

7
00:00:14,744 --> 00:00:15,704
named Alex,

8
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:17,464
with real numbers

9
00:00:23,617 --> 00:00:25,007
Alex is twenty-five,

10
00:00:25,007 --> 00:00:27,607
a software developer in a big American city

11
00:00:30,307 --> 00:00:31,417
He has a good job,

12
00:00:31,417 --> 00:00:32,497
a high salary,

13
00:00:32,497 --> 00:00:35,097
and a phone full of work emails at midnight

14
00:00:37,568 --> 00:00:38,278
Every morning,

15
00:00:38,278 --> 00:00:40,678
he spends an hour in traffic to get to

16
00:00:40,678 --> 00:00:41,438
the office,

17
00:00:41,438 --> 00:00:44,038
and another hour to get home

18
00:00:45,965 --> 00:00:48,755
His take-home pay is about six thousand dollars

19
00:00:48,755 --> 00:00:49,275
a month

20
00:00:49,275 --> 00:00:50,955
But by the end of the month,

21
00:00:50,955 --> 00:00:53,355
his account is almost empty

22
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:56,489
An expensive apartment,

23
00:00:56,489 --> 00:00:57,849
a car on finance,

24
00:00:57,849 --> 00:00:58,809
food delivery,

25
00:00:58,809 --> 00:01:01,409
and things bought just for fun

26
00:01:02,906 --> 00:01:03,776
One evening,

27
00:01:03,776 --> 00:01:05,176
Alex does the math:

28
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:06,336
living like this,

29
00:01:06,336 --> 00:01:08,936
he will have to work another forty-two years

30
00:01:11,648 --> 00:01:13,518
He looks around the office

31
00:01:13,518 --> 00:01:15,878
Colleagues in their fifties are still sitting

32
00:01:15,878 --> 00:01:16,198
there,

33
00:01:16,198 --> 00:01:16,798
tired,

34
00:01:16,798 --> 00:01:19,398
counting the days to their next holiday

35
00:01:22,135 --> 00:01:23,405
He asks himself:

36
00:01:23,405 --> 00:01:25,485
is there another way?

37
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:33,180
That night,

38
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:36,600
Alex searches online for ways to retire early

39
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,200
He stumbles into a whole new world

40
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,510
He finds an old book published in 1992,

41
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:48,110
called Your Money or Your Life

42
00:01:50,138 --> 00:01:51,648
The core idea is simple:

43
00:01:51,648 --> 00:01:54,248
money is the life energy you trade for it

44
00:01:56,578 --> 00:01:59,368
A three hundred dollar pair of shoes is not

45
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:01,328
just three hundred dollars

46
00:02:01,328 --> 00:02:03,828
It is dozens of hours of your life spent

47
00:02:03,828 --> 00:02:05,908
sitting in an office

48
00:02:07,582 --> 00:02:08,412
In 2010,

49
00:02:08,412 --> 00:02:11,132
another book argued that your savings rate is

50
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:13,732
the number that decides when you can retire

51
00:02:16,167 --> 00:02:17,277
In 2011,

52
00:02:17,277 --> 00:02:20,597
an engineer named Pete Adeney started the blog

53
00:02:20,597 --> 00:02:21,997
Mister Money Mustache

54
00:02:21,997 --> 00:02:24,597
He had quit his job at around thirty

55
00:02:27,055 --> 00:02:29,365
The blog drew millions of readers

56
00:02:29,365 --> 00:02:31,085
People shared spreadsheets,

57
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:31,765
plans,

58
00:02:31,765 --> 00:02:34,365
and stories of quitting work at thirty

59
00:02:36,778 --> 00:02:38,528
And so the movement got a name:

60
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:38,888
FIRE,

61
00:02:38,888 --> 00:02:41,088
short for Financial Independence,

62
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:42,528
Retire Early

63
00:02:50,383 --> 00:02:51,573
The first question:

64
00:02:51,573 --> 00:02:54,173
how much money do you need to stop working?

65
00:02:56,391 --> 00:02:57,861
In 1994,

66
00:02:57,861 --> 00:03:00,501
financial planner William Bengen studied

67
00:03:00,501 --> 00:03:02,901
decades of American market data

68
00:03:05,031 --> 00:03:07,241
He found that if you withdraw about four

69
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:09,501
percent of your investments each year,

70
00:03:09,501 --> 00:03:11,061
adjusted for inflation,

71
00:03:11,061 --> 00:03:14,101
historically the money almost always lasted at

72
00:03:14,101 --> 00:03:15,861
least thirty years

73
00:03:18,513 --> 00:03:20,543
Even through the worst periods,

74
00:03:20,543 --> 00:03:24,223
like the Great Depression of the 1930s,

75
00:03:24,223 --> 00:03:26,823
or the high inflation of the 1970s

76
00:03:30,369 --> 00:03:31,779
In 1998,

77
00:03:31,779 --> 00:03:35,159
a study at Trinity University reached a similar

78
00:03:35,159 --> 00:03:35,799
result

79
00:03:35,799 --> 00:03:38,399
People call it the four percent rule

80
00:03:40,274 --> 00:03:41,184
Flip it around:

81
00:03:41,184 --> 00:03:44,024
you need about twenty-five times your yearly

82
00:03:44,024 --> 00:03:45,144
spending

83
00:03:47,645 --> 00:03:49,275
Alex runs the numbers:

84
00:03:49,275 --> 00:03:52,235
living on about twenty-nine thousand dollars a

85
00:03:52,235 --> 00:03:52,595
year,

86
00:03:52,595 --> 00:03:55,555
his target is about seven hundred and twenty

87
00:03:55,555 --> 00:03:56,995
thousand dollars

88
00:03:59,284 --> 00:04:01,234
But the rule is based on the past,

89
00:04:01,234 --> 00:04:03,234
and on about thirty years

90
00:04:03,234 --> 00:04:06,314
Many early retirees choose a safer withdrawal

91
00:04:06,314 --> 00:04:06,794
rate,

92
00:04:06,794 --> 00:04:09,394
just three to three and a half percent

93
00:04:17,183 --> 00:04:20,133
The average American saves only about four to

94
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:22,533
five percent of their income

95
00:04:24,364 --> 00:04:27,034
According to a famous calculation in the FIRE

96
00:04:27,034 --> 00:04:27,914
community,

97
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:29,594
if you save ten percent,

98
00:04:29,594 --> 00:04:32,194
you need to work about fifty-one years

99
00:04:34,402 --> 00:04:36,112
Save twenty-five percent,

100
00:04:36,112 --> 00:04:38,712
and it drops to about thirty-two years

101
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:42,030
Save fifty percent,

102
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:44,630
and it is only about seventeen years

103
00:04:46,739 --> 00:04:47,649
Sixty percent:

104
00:04:47,649 --> 00:04:50,249
about twelve and a half years

105
00:04:51,808 --> 00:04:53,998
And if you save seventy-five percent,

106
00:04:53,998 --> 00:04:55,758
about seven years

107
00:04:57,804 --> 00:04:59,034
The magic is this:

108
00:04:59,034 --> 00:05:01,674
every dollar you don't spend is both saved,

109
00:05:01,674 --> 00:05:04,074
and lowers the amount you need to live on

110
00:05:04,074 --> 00:05:05,194
later

111
00:05:07,174 --> 00:05:08,164
In other words,

112
00:05:08,164 --> 00:05:11,084
someone who saves half their income can retire

113
00:05:11,084 --> 00:05:13,964
nearly thirty years earlier than someone else,

114
00:05:13,964 --> 00:05:16,044
on the same salary

115
00:05:18,366 --> 00:05:19,516
Alex sets a goal:

116
00:05:19,516 --> 00:05:21,636
save sixty percent of his income

117
00:05:21,636 --> 00:05:22,676
If he can do it,

118
00:05:22,676 --> 00:05:25,276
he could be free in about thirteen years

119
00:05:33,617 --> 00:05:36,127
Alex doesn't start by cutting out coffee

120
00:05:36,127 --> 00:05:38,567
He goes after the three biggest costs:

121
00:05:38,567 --> 00:05:39,207
housing,

122
00:05:39,207 --> 00:05:40,047
transport,

123
00:05:40,047 --> 00:05:41,487
and food

124
00:05:43,277 --> 00:05:45,667
He moves into a smaller apartment with a

125
00:05:45,667 --> 00:05:46,067
friend,

126
00:05:46,067 --> 00:05:48,667
cutting his rent almost in half

127
00:05:51,391 --> 00:05:53,461
He sells the car he was paying off,

128
00:05:53,461 --> 00:05:54,581
buys a bicycle,

129
00:05:54,581 --> 00:05:57,181
and takes the train when it rains

130
00:05:59,788 --> 00:06:01,898
Cycling every day makes him healthier,

131
00:06:01,898 --> 00:06:04,498
and saves him the gym membership too

132
00:06:06,447 --> 00:06:07,557
He learns to cook,

133
00:06:07,557 --> 00:06:08,997
brings lunch to work,

134
00:06:08,997 --> 00:06:11,597
and only eats out on weekends

135
00:06:13,548 --> 00:06:14,618
For everything else,

136
00:06:14,618 --> 00:06:16,298
he asks one question:

137
00:06:16,298 --> 00:06:18,658
is this worth the hours of work it costs

138
00:06:18,658 --> 00:06:19,778
me?

139
00:06:21,372 --> 00:06:23,882
He cancels subscriptions he never uses,

140
00:06:23,882 --> 00:06:26,522
and waits thirty days before buying anything

141
00:06:26,522 --> 00:06:27,642
expensive

142
00:06:29,907 --> 00:06:30,897
After one year,

143
00:06:30,897 --> 00:06:33,297
Alex has saved more than forty thousand

144
00:06:33,297 --> 00:06:33,977
dollars

145
00:06:33,977 --> 00:06:35,217
And the surprise:

146
00:06:35,217 --> 00:06:37,817
he doesn't feel miserable at all

147
00:06:45,583 --> 00:06:48,173
But money sitting in a savings account gets

148
00:06:48,173 --> 00:06:50,013
eaten away by inflation

149
00:06:50,013 --> 00:06:52,613
Alex needs to put his money to work

150
00:06:54,847 --> 00:06:56,477
In 1976,

151
00:06:56,477 --> 00:06:59,477
John Bogle launched the first index fund for

152
00:06:59,477 --> 00:07:00,917
individual investors

153
00:07:03,742 --> 00:07:05,912
Instead of trying to pick winning stocks,

154
00:07:05,912 --> 00:07:08,492
an index fund buys the whole market,

155
00:07:08,492 --> 00:07:10,892
at a very low cost

156
00:07:12,295 --> 00:07:13,485
Over the long run,

157
00:07:13,485 --> 00:07:15,525
most actively managed funds,

158
00:07:15,525 --> 00:07:17,765
despite charging higher fees,

159
00:07:17,765 --> 00:07:20,365
still fail to beat the market index

160
00:07:22,897 --> 00:07:25,287
It is the FIRE community's favourite tool:

161
00:07:25,287 --> 00:07:25,847
simple,

162
00:07:25,847 --> 00:07:26,367
cheap,

163
00:07:26,367 --> 00:07:28,967
and no need to guess the market

164
00:07:30,566 --> 00:07:33,916
Alex automatically moves money into index funds

165
00:07:33,916 --> 00:07:34,996
every month,

166
00:07:34,996 --> 00:07:37,476
prioritising tax-advantaged retirement

167
00:07:37,476 --> 00:07:38,596
accounts

168
00:07:40,607 --> 00:07:43,157
He doesn't check stock prices every day

169
00:07:43,157 --> 00:07:45,717
He only looks at his account a few times

170
00:07:45,717 --> 00:07:47,157
a year

171
00:07:49,272 --> 00:07:50,342
In the first year,

172
00:07:50,342 --> 00:07:52,222
the gains are barely noticeable

173
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:54,742
But compound interest is like a snowball:

174
00:07:54,742 --> 00:07:56,182
the longer it rolls,

175
00:07:56,182 --> 00:07:58,262
the faster it grows

176
00:08:06,183 --> 00:08:07,213
In 2020,

177
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:08,813
Alex is twenty-eight

178
00:08:08,813 --> 00:08:11,293
His investments have grown to nearly two

179
00:08:11,293 --> 00:08:13,053
hundred thousand dollars

180
00:08:15,327 --> 00:08:16,957
Then the pandemic hits

181
00:08:16,957 --> 00:08:18,477
In about one month,

182
00:08:18,477 --> 00:08:20,357
the American stock index,

183
00:08:20,357 --> 00:08:22,317
the S&P 500,

184
00:08:22,317 --> 00:08:24,397
plunges about thirty-four percent

185
00:08:27,376 --> 00:08:30,006
Nearly seventy thousand dollars of Alex's

186
00:08:30,006 --> 00:08:32,086
savings vanishes on paper

187
00:08:34,429 --> 00:08:35,869
Friends panic and sell

188
00:08:35,869 --> 00:08:38,269
Alex wants to sell too

189
00:08:40,608 --> 00:08:42,518
Every night he opens the app,

190
00:08:42,518 --> 00:08:44,278
stares at the red numbers,

191
00:08:44,278 --> 00:08:46,038
and can't sleep

192
00:08:47,836 --> 00:08:49,266
But he remembers the rule:

193
00:08:49,266 --> 00:08:50,746
never sell in a panic,

194
00:08:50,746 --> 00:08:53,346
and keep buying steadily every month

195
00:08:56,212 --> 00:08:58,242
Only about five months later,

196
00:08:58,242 --> 00:09:00,082
the market hits a new high

197
00:09:00,082 --> 00:09:02,682
Those who sold missed the recovery

198
00:09:04,706 --> 00:09:06,256
That is the hardest test:

199
00:09:06,256 --> 00:09:07,056
not maths,

200
00:09:07,056 --> 00:09:08,496
but psychology

201
00:09:16,217 --> 00:09:17,327
The more he learns,

202
00:09:17,327 --> 00:09:19,967
the more Alex sees that FIRE comes in more

203
00:09:19,967 --> 00:09:21,727
than one flavour

204
00:09:23,294 --> 00:09:24,244
On the forums,

205
00:09:24,244 --> 00:09:25,804
people argue endlessly:

206
00:09:25,804 --> 00:09:27,884
how much is enough?

207
00:09:29,177 --> 00:09:29,947
Lean FIRE:

208
00:09:29,947 --> 00:09:31,407
live very frugally,

209
00:09:31,407 --> 00:09:32,527
need less money,

210
00:09:32,527 --> 00:09:34,607
and become free sooner

211
00:09:36,273 --> 00:09:36,903
Fat FIRE:

212
00:09:36,903 --> 00:09:38,823
build enough to live comfortably,

213
00:09:38,823 --> 00:09:39,783
even lavishly,

214
00:09:39,783 --> 00:09:41,543
but work longer

215
00:09:43,904 --> 00:09:44,734
Barista FIRE:

216
00:09:44,734 --> 00:09:46,094
leave full-time work,

217
00:09:46,094 --> 00:09:47,574
take a part-time job,

218
00:09:47,574 --> 00:09:48,854
like making coffee,

219
00:09:48,854 --> 00:09:51,454
for some income and health insurance

220
00:09:54,244 --> 00:09:54,994
Coast FIRE:

221
00:09:54,994 --> 00:09:56,314
save hard early on,

222
00:09:56,314 --> 00:09:58,914
then let compound interest do the rest,

223
00:09:58,914 --> 00:10:01,514
while you only earn enough to live

224
00:10:03,723 --> 00:10:06,473
Each one is a trade-off between years at work

225
00:10:06,473 --> 00:10:09,073
and your standard of living after you stop

226
00:10:11,431 --> 00:10:13,261
Alex chooses the middle path:

227
00:10:13,261 --> 00:10:14,661
enough to live comfortably,

228
00:10:14,661 --> 00:10:16,421
but not lavishly

229
00:10:23,683 --> 00:10:24,433
In America,

230
00:10:24,433 --> 00:10:27,233
the biggest challenge for early retirees is

231
00:10:27,233 --> 00:10:28,273
health insurance

232
00:10:28,273 --> 00:10:29,113
Medicare,

233
00:10:29,113 --> 00:10:31,033
the program for older people,

234
00:10:31,033 --> 00:10:33,113
only starts at sixty-five

235
00:10:35,659 --> 00:10:37,048
In Britain or Germany,

236
00:10:37,048 --> 00:10:39,288
public healthcare carries that burden

237
00:10:39,288 --> 00:10:42,208
But salaries are often lower and taxes higher,

238
00:10:42,208 --> 00:10:44,288
so saving is harder

239
00:10:46,313 --> 00:10:47,063
In Japan,

240
00:10:47,063 --> 00:10:49,903
the FIRE trend took off around 2020,

241
00:10:49,903 --> 00:10:52,583
especially among young people worn out by a

242
00:10:52,583 --> 00:10:54,343
culture of overtime

243
00:10:56,919 --> 00:10:57,869
In Australia,

244
00:10:57,869 --> 00:11:01,189
compulsory superannuation can only be accessed

245
00:11:01,189 --> 00:11:02,389
from age sixty,

246
00:11:02,389 --> 00:11:05,469
so FIRE followers must build extra savings for

247
00:11:05,469 --> 00:11:07,549
the years before that

248
00:11:09,430 --> 00:11:10,140
In America,

249
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:12,820
tax-advantaged retirement accounts usually

250
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:15,140
charge a penalty for withdrawals before

251
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:16,620
fifty-nine and a half,

252
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:19,380
so FIRE followers must plan carefully how to

253
00:11:19,380 --> 00:11:21,140
draw their money

254
00:11:22,951 --> 00:11:25,421
Some even choose to live in countries with a

255
00:11:25,421 --> 00:11:26,741
lower cost of living,

256
00:11:26,741 --> 00:11:29,141
so their savings go further

257
00:11:31,636 --> 00:11:33,666
Every country has its own rules,

258
00:11:33,666 --> 00:11:35,346
but the formula is the same:

259
00:11:35,346 --> 00:11:36,786
spend less than you earn,

260
00:11:36,786 --> 00:11:38,266
invest the difference,

261
00:11:38,266 --> 00:11:40,026
and be patient

262
00:11:47,517 --> 00:11:48,987
Thirteen years pass

263
00:11:48,987 --> 00:11:50,747
Alex is thirty-eight

264
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:55,090
His investments have passed the target:

265
00:11:55,090 --> 00:11:57,490
about eight hundred thousand dollars

266
00:11:59,828 --> 00:12:01,698
He hands in his resignation

267
00:12:01,698 --> 00:12:04,298
His colleagues think he won the lottery

268
00:12:06,399 --> 00:12:08,109
The farewell party has cake,

269
00:12:08,109 --> 00:12:09,109
kind words,

270
00:12:09,109 --> 00:12:10,709
and a few doubtful looks:

271
00:12:10,709 --> 00:12:12,789
will he regret it?

272
00:12:15,088 --> 00:12:16,398
On his first Monday,

273
00:12:16,398 --> 00:12:18,238
Alex doesn't set an alarm

274
00:12:18,238 --> 00:12:19,758
He cycles to the lake,

275
00:12:19,758 --> 00:12:20,958
sits with a coffee,

276
00:12:20,958 --> 00:12:23,558
and watches the crowds rushing to work

277
00:12:26,590 --> 00:12:28,940
He moves part of his money into cash,

278
00:12:28,940 --> 00:12:30,860
enough to live on for two years,

279
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:33,060
so he won't have to sell shares when the

280
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:34,500
market falls

281
00:12:36,814 --> 00:12:38,044
In the first months,

282
00:12:38,044 --> 00:12:38,964
he travels,

283
00:12:38,964 --> 00:12:39,644
reads,

284
00:12:39,644 --> 00:12:41,244
learns to play the guitar,

285
00:12:41,244 --> 00:12:43,644
and finally gets enough sleep

286
00:12:45,629 --> 00:12:46,179
But then,

287
00:12:46,179 --> 00:12:48,779
something he never expected begins to appear

288
00:12:57,183 --> 00:12:58,353
After six months,

289
00:12:58,353 --> 00:13:00,493
Alex starts to feel empty

290
00:13:00,493 --> 00:13:02,453
His friends are all at work

291
00:13:02,453 --> 00:13:05,021
No one asks what he does for a living

292
00:13:05,021 --> 00:13:06,141
anymore

293
00:13:08,750 --> 00:13:11,940
Many early retirees describe feeling lost,

294
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:12,540
lonely,

295
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:15,140
and losing part of their identity

296
00:13:17,370 --> 00:13:19,040
Then come the money worries

297
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:21,520
If the market crashes in the first years of

298
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:22,280
retirement,

299
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:24,920
the money can run out faster than planned

300
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:27,520
This is called sequence of returns risk

301
00:13:30,885 --> 00:13:32,235
Inflation rises,

302
00:13:32,235 --> 00:13:34,715
and healthcare in America keeps getting more

303
00:13:34,715 --> 00:13:35,835
expensive

304
00:13:38,072 --> 00:13:40,022
Alex has sleepless nights too,

305
00:13:40,022 --> 00:13:42,982
wondering if seven hundred and twenty thousand

306
00:13:42,982 --> 00:13:45,062
dollars is really enough

307
00:13:47,313 --> 00:13:49,863
Quite a few FIRE followers have gone back to

308
00:13:49,863 --> 00:13:50,223
work,

309
00:13:50,223 --> 00:13:51,343
partly for the money,

310
00:13:51,343 --> 00:13:53,943
and partly because they want something to do

311
00:13:56,559 --> 00:13:58,429
And FIRE is not for everyone

312
00:13:58,429 --> 00:14:00,309
For people on low incomes,

313
00:14:00,309 --> 00:14:02,909
just covering the bills is already hard

314
00:14:10,917 --> 00:14:13,147
Alex doesn't go back to the office

315
00:14:13,147 --> 00:14:15,347
But he discovers something important:

316
00:14:15,347 --> 00:14:17,747
freedom doesn't mean doing nothing

317
00:14:20,137 --> 00:14:22,567
He teaches coding to students for free,

318
00:14:22,567 --> 00:14:25,127
works on a few small projects he loves,

319
00:14:25,127 --> 00:14:27,727
and volunteers at a community garden

320
00:14:30,877 --> 00:14:33,067
He spends more time with his parents,

321
00:14:33,067 --> 00:14:33,947
his friends,

322
00:14:33,947 --> 00:14:36,347
and on his own health

323
00:14:37,756 --> 00:14:40,226
That small income lets him withdraw less from

324
00:14:40,226 --> 00:14:41,386
his investments,

325
00:14:41,386 --> 00:14:43,786
and helps him feel useful

326
00:14:45,579 --> 00:14:46,249
For Alex,

327
00:14:46,249 --> 00:14:49,009
FIRE isn't about running away from work

328
00:14:49,009 --> 00:14:51,609
It is about having the freedom to choose

329
00:14:54,519 --> 00:14:56,389
According to a recent survey,

330
00:14:56,389 --> 00:14:59,109
about thirty-seven percent of Americans say

331
00:14:59,109 --> 00:15:00,709
they are interested in FIRE,

332
00:15:00,709 --> 00:15:03,309
a big jump from the year before

333
00:15:05,423 --> 00:15:07,973
Maybe you don't want to retire at forty

334
00:15:07,973 --> 00:15:10,573
But Alex's question is still worth thinking

335
00:15:10,573 --> 00:15:11,013
about:

336
00:15:11,013 --> 00:15:13,613
what are you trading your life's time for?

337
00:15:18,450 --> 00:15:19,920
Whatever path you choose,

338
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:21,920
the lessons of FIRE still help:

339
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:23,520
know where your money goes,

340
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:24,400
save early,

341
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:25,600
invest steadily,

342
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,360
and don't panic

343
00:15:28,125 --> 00:15:29,995
Alex is a fictional character,

344
00:15:29,995 --> 00:15:32,315
but the numbers and the story of the FIRE

345
00:15:32,315 --> 00:15:33,515
movement are real

346
00:15:33,515 --> 00:15:35,915
This is not financial advice

347
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:38,160
If you could choose,

348
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:39,840
at what age would you retire?

349
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:42,440
Let me know in the comments
